### Page 19 of 20

```text
The Jolly Rogers redevelopment proposes development of 194 multifamily residential units on the
eastern portion of the project area. On April 18, 2024, the Board of Adjustment approved a conditional
use request to allow multi-family at this location.

Based on similar construction styles in the community, the developer estimates the increment
generated for this project to be $250,000 annually.  The Plan and Project proposes authorizing TIF
expenses to:
1. Aide in site acquisition costs;
2. Relocate utilities as necessary, specifically the water and sewer lines on the southern property
line;
3. Improve stormwater facilities on the site;
4. Remediate any environmental contamination;
5. Improve any streets and curb, gutter, and sidewalks;
6. Facilitate economic development and site beautification.

Staff has reviewed the requests for consistency with Wyo. Stat. § 15-9-113 and the Wyoming
Constitution (Specifically, Article 16, Section 6 (a) (i) and Article 3, Section 36).

Relevant sections of the State Constitution are as follows:
•
Article 16, Section 6, Loan of credit; donations prohibited; works of internal improvement.
“Neither the state nor any county, city, township, town, school district, or any other political
subdivision, shall . . . loan or give its credit or make donations to or in aid of any individual,
association or corporation, except for necessary support of the poor . . .”
•
Article 3, Section 36, Prohibited appropriations.
“No appropriation shall be made for charitable, industrial, educational or benevolent purposes
to any person, corporation or community not under the absolute control of the state, nor to any
denominational or sectarian institution or association.”

The above-mentioned sections of the State Constitution prohibit a political subdivision from loaning or
gifting funding to a private individual, corporation, or similar entity unless they clearly serve a public
purpose.

Wyoming’s Urban Renewal program has been dusted off by the City of Cheyenne after decades of
inactivity in the State. Cheyenne is taking the lead and other communities are watching closely.
Cheyenne has been careful with the programs within the Urban Renewal Statutes (Urban Renewal and
Downtown Development Authority) to ensure that all funds passing through the programs have a clear
public purpose.

Staff believes that requested items 2, 3, 4, and 5 clearly meet the intent of Wyoming’s Urban Renewal
Statutes and the State Constitution. Staff has concerns with the entirety of request number 1, the
economic development provisions identified in section 6, and the vagueness of site beautification
identified in section 6. The Plan and Project document does not clearly identify how items 1 and 6
clearly serve a public purpose.

Overall, approval of the plan will help aide redevelopment within an area determined as slum and blight.
The City plans to use tax increment financing (TIF), enabled by Wyo. Stat. § 15-9-120, to aid in the
completion of the requested objectives as conditioned in this approval.
```

